* {{IPA|en|/ˈbɐʉ.nəs/|a=NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊnəs|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|bo|nus}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|+|bonusses|boni<l:nonstandard>}}

# Something [[extra]] that is good; an added benefit.
# An extra sum given as a [[premium]], e.g. to an employee or to a shareholder.
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=February 14, 2009|title=Letters: Time to sack the bonus culture|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/business/2009/feb/14/bonus-culture-labour
|passage=I was a bank manager in the 1970s, but I never received or expected to receive a '''bonus''' for doing my paid work.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20241226164305/https://www.theguardian.com/business/2009/feb/14/bonus-culture-labour}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=70
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21579879-buy-out-firm-really-does-focus-operational-improvements-engineers|title=Engineers of a different kind
|passage=Private-equity nabobs bristle at being dubbed mere financiers.{{...}}Much of their pleading is public-relations bluster. Clever financial ploys are what have made billionaires of the industry’s veterans. “Operational improvement” in a portfolio company has often meant little more than promising colossal '''bonuses''' to sitting chief executives if they meet ambitious growth targets. That model is still prevalent today.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170608005531/http://www.economist.com:80/news/finance-and-economics/21579879-buy-out-firm-really-does-focus-operational-improvements-engineers|archivedate=8 June 2017}}
#: {{ux|en|The employee of the week receives a '''bonus''' for his excellent work.}}
# {{lb|en|video games}} An addition to the [[player]]'s [[score]] based on [[performance]], e.g. for time remaining.
# {{lb|en|basketball}} One or more [[free throw]]s awarded to a team when the opposing team has accumulated enough [[foul]]s.

